course: biology
lesson: 182
title: Deserts of the world: fennec foxes, fog-drinking beetles, camels and silver ants
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A desert is a place where very little rain falls, so the hardest problem for every living thing there is water, and in hot deserts the second problem is heat.
explain: Desert animals solve it in clever ways: some drink fog, some get all their water from food, some hide in burrows by day and come out at night.
explain: Think of the hot sand on a beach in August that burns your feet: a sidewinder snake lifts most of its body off the sand, and silver ants run on long legs so their bodies stay above the hottest layer.
explain: Desert plants save water too: a cactus stores it in a fat stem and swells after rain, like a sponge you fill and then squeeze slowly for months.
fact: The fennec fox of the Sahara is the smallest fox, with the biggest ears for its size of any fox; the ears let out heat and hear insects moving under the sand.
fact: Fennec foxes can go a long time without drinking, because they get water from their food.
fact: The fog-basking beetle of the Namib Desert stands head-down on a dune at dawn; fog forms droplets on its bumpy back that roll down to its mouth, and engineers have copied its back to collect water from fog.
fact: The sidewinder snake moves sideways and touches the hot sand at only two points, leaving J-shaped tracks.
fact: Camels can go many days without drinking; their humps store fat, not water, and a thirsty camel can drink over a hundred litres in about ten minutes.
fact: Saharan silver ants come out at the hottest time of day to find animals killed by the heat, while the lizards that hunt them hide from it; silver hairs reflect sunlight, and they can run nearly a metre a second.
fact: Welwitschia, a plant of the Namib Desert, has only two leaves its whole life; they keep growing from the base and the wind tears them into strips, and some plants may be over a thousand years old.
fact: Kangaroo rats of North American deserts can live without ever drinking, getting water from seeds and making very dry urine.
fact: Horned lizards can squirt blood from their eyes at dogs, foxes and coyotes; the blood tastes bad to them, but the lizards do not do it to birds.
fact: A cactus stores water in its stem and swells after rain; its spines are changed leaves that shade it and stop animals eating it, and a big saguaro can hold over a thousand litres.
fact: The Gila monster is a venomous lizard that stores fat in its thick tail and eats big meals only a few times a year; a medicine for diabetes was made from a chemical in its saliva.
fact: A real roadrunner can run about thirty kilometres an hour, but a coyote can run about twice as fast.
fact: Male sandgrouse soak their belly feathers at a waterhole and fly many kilometres back to their chicks, which drink from the wet feathers.
exercise: What are a fennec fox's huge ears for? | Letting out heat and hearing insects under the sand | Storing water for the long dry summer like a camel's hump | Scaring lions | Digging burrows
exercise: Which is the smallest fox in the world? | The fennec fox | The common red fox | The white Arctic fox | The American grey fox
exercise: How does the Namib fog-basking beetle get water? | Fog forms droplets on its back that roll down to its mouth | It digs deep wells in the sand | It drinks from cactus flowers | It licks the sweat and water off passing camels in the morning
exercise: Why does the sidewinder move sideways? | It touches the hot sand at only a few points and grips loose sand | It cannot see forwards, so it watches the sand behind it instead | It is chasing its own tail | Its head is too heavy
exercise: What does a camel's hump store? | Fat | Water | Warm air | Milk
exercise: How much can a very thirsty camel drink in about ten minutes? | Over a hundred litres | One small glass | About two litres | A whole lake of water in a minute
exercise: Why do Saharan silver ants come out at midday? | To find animals killed by the heat, when their enemies are hiding | Because they cannot see anything at night, not even with a full moon | Because the sand is coolest then | To sunbathe
exercise: What keeps silver ants from overheating? | Silver hairs that reflect sunlight and long legs | A thick shell full of cool water inside its body | Sweating a lot to stay cool in the sun | Hiding inside camels
exercise: How many leaves does a welwitschia have in its whole life? | Two | None | Hundreds | A new one every day
exercise: How does a kangaroo rat live without drinking? | It gets water from seeds and makes very dry urine | It drinks dew every night | It never needs any water at all, not even in its food | It stores water in its tail
exercise: Why does a horned lizard squirt blood from its eyes? | The blood tastes bad to dogs, foxes and coyotes | To cool its hot eyes down in the burning midday heat | To attract a mate | To wash the sand out of its eyes after a storm
exercise: What are cactus spines? | Changed leaves that shade the plant and protect it | Hairs for drinking up the rain whenever it falls on the plant | Roots growing the wrong way | Seeds waiting to fall
exercise: Why does a cactus get fatter after rain? | It stores the water in its stem | Its spines grow longer and sharper | It catches and eats more insects | It fills up with air like a balloon
exercise: Where does a Gila monster store food for hard times? | As fat in its thick tail | In pouches inside its cheeks | In a burrow full of stored eggs | In a fatty hump on its back
exercise: What medicine was made from a chemical in Gila monster saliva? | A medicine for diabetes | A medicine for colds | A sun cream | A cure for snakebite and scorpion stings
exercise: How do sandgrouse chicks get water? | Their father soaks his belly feathers and they drink from them | They fly to the waterhole on their first day | They lick dew off rocks | Their mother carries water in her beak
test: In the cartoon Road Runner always zooms away from Wile E. Coyote. What is true about the real animals? | A real coyote can run faster than a real roadrunner | Roadrunners are faster than cheetahs | Coyotes never hunt roadrunners at all in the real desert | Roadrunners cannot run, only fly
test: «camel humps r full of water, everyone knows that» What is right? | No, the humps store fat; camels save water in many other ways | Yes, each hump is a water tank that they fill when they drink | Yes, they fill them from rivers | No, the humps are full of air
test: «a camel with 2 humps and 2 camels with 1 hump each, how many humps» | 4 | 3 | 5 | 6
test: «5 fennec foxes in the zoo, count the ears» | 10 | 5 | 20 | 15
test: In Zootopia Finnick the fennec fox has a deep voice and big ears. Which part of the real animal is right? | The huge ears, which let out heat and catch the sound of insects | The deep voice, which scares lions | Fennecs drive vans in the desert | Fennecs live in cities
test: What would happen if a thick fog stopped coming to the Namib Desert? | Fog-drinking beetles and plants that catch fog would lose their water | The beetles would start drinking from the sea | Nothing, because beetles never need water | The desert would turn into a forest
test: «dog barked at a lizard and it shot blood out its eyes, gross» What is going on? | It is a horned lizard defending itself with bad-tasting blood | The lizard is badly ill | The dog bit it | The lizard is crying because it is sad and lonely in the dry desert
test: A sidewinder leaves strange tracks in the sand. What shape are they? | Short J-shaped marks side by side | One long straight line in the sand | Round holes | Zigzag footprints
test: Why might a desert animal hide in a burrow by day and come out at night? | It is much cooler at night, so it loses less water | It is afraid of the moon | Food only grows at night | It cannot see in daylight because the sun is far too bright for its eyes
test: «my uncle said cactus spines are just thorns from the stem» What is right? | No, the spines are changed leaves | Yes, they grow out of the stem bark | No, they are tiny roots | Yes, they are dead flowers
test: In Rango, a chameleon walks into a desert town looking for water. Why is water the main worry in a desert? | Very little rain falls, so water is the hardest thing to find | Deserts are always flooded | Animals in deserts never drink | The sand is full of rivers running under it, so water is easy to find
exam: Identify the substance which is principally stored within the hump of a camel. | Fat | Water | Blood | Just air
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the method by which the Namib fog-basking beetle obtains water? | Droplets condense from fog upon its back and run down to its mouth | It excavates subterranean wells | It extracts moisture exclusively from cactus stems | It consumes the blood of larger animals
exam: Ascertain the function of the spines borne by a cactus. | They are modified leaves which shade the plant and deter herbivores | They absorb rainwater directly from the air | They serve as organs of reproduction | They are roots which have emerged above ground
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the kangaroo rat. | It may live without drinking, obtaining water from seeds and producing very dry urine | It drinks several litres of water daily | It stores water in its tail | It inhabits rainforests exclusively
